. Structure (biggest win)
Split the single tracker app. At the moment it holds auth, interviews, learning, mock AI, placements and attendance. I'd suggest separate apps:
accounts: users, roles, OTP registration.
interviews: interviews, rounds, notes.
learning: courses and the mock interview bank.
placements: placed students, selections, banners.
attendance: attendance and leave.
Break up views.py and models.py. views.py is 2,484 lines and models.py is 513. Make each a package with one module per feature, e.g. views/attendance.py.
Add a services.py layer. Business rules such as record_placement, leave validation and attendance marking currently live in views, models and forms. Moving them to services makes them reusable and easier to test. Views then only handle HTTP.
Replace the repeated permission logic. The owner = created_by if is_hr else user line appears in many places. Put it in one helper such as user.data_owner, or a small mixin or manager like Group.objects.for_user(user).
Move the decorators into permissions.py.
2. Data and performance
Add indexes on the fields you filter on most: InterviewStatus.updated_at, Attendance.date, LeaveRequest.status and PlacedStudent.company.
Avoid N+1 queries. Only 28 select_related/prefetch_related calls cover about 105 views and classes. I'd audit the list pages for this.
Cache the banner query. The context processor runs on every page for every user. Cache it for 5–10 minutes (Django's cache framework with a short TTL), and invalidate it when a placement is saved.
Use Company and Role lookup tables. Company names are free text, which is why "Deloitte" and "deloitte" can appear as different entries. A normalised company table would also give you clean placement statistics.
Keep the database changes in migrations. There are about 34 of them. I'd squash the early ones once everything is deployed.
3. Reliability
Apply migrations on deploy. On Vercel nothing runs them automatically. Add a build step or a documented release command, otherwise code can ship before its tables exist.
Move OTP email and the AI mock interview calls to a background queue (Celery or similar, or a managed queue if you stay on Vercel). Serverless request timeouts are a risk for AI calls.
Rate-limit sensitive endpoints: OTP send/verify and login. Use django-ratelimit or similar.
Use structured logging and Sentry for error tracking. Production issues are currently hard to see.
Move media and uploads to object storage (S3 or similar) if any exist. Vercel's filesystem is ephemeral.
4. Quality
Split tests.py. It is 1,880 lines in one file and hangs when run as a whole. Make it a tests/ package per feature, use setUpTestData, and run in parallel (--parallel). Find out why the suite hangs, since CI is already set up in tests.yml.
Add lint and format tools (ruff, plus pre-commit) and type hints on the service layer.
Add a Makefile or scripts for run/test/migrate, and update the README with the architecture.
Remove stray files from the repo root. improvements, suggestions, mock_interview_questions and env are untracked or unclear, and the spreadsheet with phone numbers shouldn't sit in the project folder. Add them to .gitignore or move them.
5. Frontend
Split app.css (1,078 lines) into base, layout, components and per-feature files. Some of its rules are minified onto single lines (for example the .dir-summary rule), so it needs a formatter.
Extract repeated template markup (page heading, status badge, table, empty state) into {% include %} partials or template tags.
Replace the inline JS in templates with small static modules.
Use a hashed static filename (WhiteNoise's manifest storage) in place of the manual ?v=N cache-busting.
6. Security and privacy
Check the student personal data (phone numbers and so on). Restrict who can export it and consider masking.
Review every POST action for object-level ownership. I did this for the new leave and attendance views. The older views should be audited the same way.
Add audit logging for admin actions such as deleting a student or approving a leave.
Suggested order
Migration and deploy step, then rate limiting and error tracking. These are quick and protect production.
Fix and split the tests so refactors are safe.
Split views.py and models.py into packages and add the owner helper.
Add indexes, the query audit and banner caching.
Introduce the Company table.
Apply the CSS and template clean-up.
Split into separate apps and add background jobs. This is the largest change and should come last.
